lesson_id = "sort_quick"
title = "Quick Sort"
category = "data_structures"
icon_text = "⚡"
prerequisites = Array[String](["sort_insertion"])
visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_ql84h")
concept_text = "Quick Sort is one of the FASTEST sorting algorithms in practice. It uses a \"divide and conquer\" strategy: pick a PIVOT, split the array into \"smaller than pivot\" and \"larger than pivot\", then recursively sort each side.

How it works on [3, 6, 8, 10, 1, 2, 1]:

Step 1 — Pick a pivot. (Common choices: first, last, middle, or random.) Let's say we pick the middle: 10.

Step 2 — Partition. Move all items smaller than 10 to the LEFT, and all items larger than 10 to the RIGHT.

Left (smaller):  [3, 6, 8, 1, 2, 1]
Pivot:            [10]
Right (larger):  []

Step 3 — Recursively sort the LEFT and RIGHT sides.

Sort left [3, 6, 8, 1, 2, 1]:
- Pick pivot: 8
- Left:  [3, 6, 1, 2, 1]
- Pivot: [8]
- Right: []

Sort [3, 6, 1, 2, 1]:
- Pick pivot: 1
- Left:  []
- Pivot: [1]
- Right: [3, 6, 2]

And so on, until each piece has just one item (which is trivially sorted).

Step 4 — Combine everything back together.

Final: [1, 1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 10]

Performance:
- Average case: O(n log n) — FAST
- Worst case: O(n²) — rare, happens with bad pivot choices (eg always picking smallest/largest)
- Space: O(log n) for the recursion stack
- NOT stable: equal elements may swap order
- Used in MANY real-world libraries (under the name \"introsort\" combined with heapsort)
- One of the most commonly used sorts in practice"

real_world_example = "Imagine organizing a bookshelf full of mixed books. You want to sort them by height.

Step 1: Pick a book to use as a reference (the pivot). Say you pick a book in the middle.

Step 2: Walk down the shelf. Put every book SHORTER than the pivot on the LEFT, and every book TALLER on the RIGHT. The pivot goes in the middle.

Now you have two smaller piles (left and right of the pivot). Each is still messy, but smaller.

Step 3: Repeat the process on each pile. Pick a pivot in each, split into smaller/taller, recurse.

Eventually, you have piles of just one book each. One book is always \"sorted\". Combine everything back together, and the whole shelf is sorted by height.

Another example: a tournament bracket. You start with 8 teams. Each game, two teams play, and the winner moves on. After 3 rounds (log₂(8) = 3), you have a single champion. Quick Sort does something similar with numbers — at each level, it groups them by \"smaller than pivot\" and \"larger than pivot\", then recurses on each group.

A third example: how some card games work. You split the deck into \"red cards\" and \"black cards\" (partition by a property), then sort each pile separately, then combine.

Why use Quick Sort? Because it's FAST in practice. On average, it does n log n work. For 1 million items, that's about 20 million operations — fast. The worst case (n²) is rare if you pick pivots wisely (like random or median-of-three)."

guided_example = "Let's trace Quick Sort on [3, 6, 8, 10, 1, 2, 1], step by step. This is recursive, so we'll go level by level.

Level 1 — Sort the whole array.

Pick a pivot. Let's pick the middle: arr[3] = 10.

Partition: put everything smaller than 10 on the left, everything larger on the right.

Smaller: [3, 6, 8, 1, 2, 1]   (6 items, all less than 10)
Pivot:    [10]
Larger:   []                     (nothing greater than 10)

Array: [3, 6, 8, 1, 2, 1, 10]

The 10 is now in its final position. We never touch it again.

Level 2 — Recursively sort the smaller part [3, 6, 8, 1, 2, 1].

Pick a pivot. Middle of this sub-array: arr[2] = 8 (well, the 3rd item in this part).

Partition:
Smaller: [3, 6, 1, 2, 1]   (all less than 8)
Pivot:    [8]
Larger:   []

Array: [3, 6, 1, 2, 1, 8, 10]

Level 3 — Sort [3, 6, 1, 2, 1].

Pick pivot: 1 (the middle).

Partition:
Smaller: []
Pivot:    [1]
Larger:   [3, 6, 2]

Array: [1, 3, 6, 2, 8, 10]

Level 4 — Sort the right sub-array [3, 6, 2].

Pick pivot: 3.

Partition:
Smaller: [2]
Pivot:    [3]
Larger:   [6]

Array: [1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 10]

Level 5 — Sort [6]. Already a single item, so it's \"sorted\".

Final: [1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 10]. SORTED!

The key insight: at each level, the pivot ends up in its FINAL position. Then we recursively sort the two sides. The total work is roughly n log n because the array gets cut roughly in half at each level."

recap_text = "- Quick Sort uses divide-and-conquer: pick pivot, partition, recurse
- PIVOT choice matters — random or \"median of three\" avoids worst case
- Partition: smaller items go left, larger items go right
- After partition, the pivot is in its final position
- Recursively sort the left and right halves
- Average: O(n log n) — very fast
- Worst: O(n²) — rare with good pivot choice
- Space: O(log n) for the recursion stack
- NOT stable: equal elements may change order
- One of the fastest sorts in practice
- Used in: many real-world libraries (often as part of \"introsort\")
- Recursive — each level splits the array roughly in half"

